# Categories 6 to 10 Rights Data
rights_cat_6_to_10 = [
    # Cat 6: Family & Personal Matters (id: 6)
    (601, 6, 'Right to Claim Monthly Maintenance for Spouses and Children', 'maintenance-claim-crpc-125-bnss',
     'Spouses, children, and parents unable to maintain themselves have the statutory right to monthly maintenance.',
     'If your spouse has deserted or neglects you and you cannot support yourself, the court can order them to pay monthly maintenance based on their income.',
     'Section 125 of CrPC (Section 144 BNSS) and Hindu Adoptions and Maintenance Act 1956 provide speedy monthly maintenance. Supreme Court in Rajnesh v. Neha mandated comprehensive income disclosure.',
     'Dependent spouses, minor children, major children with disability, and elderly parents.',
     'Prevents destitution and homelessness among vulnerable family members.',
     'A dependent mother of two was deserted without financial support. The Family Court ordered interim maintenance of Rs. 25,000/month within 60 days based on the husband salary slip.',
     '1. File an application under Section 125 CrPC / 144 BNSS in Family Court.\n2. Submit comprehensive Income & Asset Affidavit as per Supreme Court format.\n3. Seek interim maintenance during pendency of trial.\n4. Court passes monthly maintenance order executable through salary attachment.',
     'Marriage certificate, Children birth certificates, Income and Asset Affidavit, Bank statements, Proof of spouse income.',
     'Family Court / Judicial Magistrate', 'eCourts Services Portal (ecourts.gov.in)',
     '1. Application filed. 2. Notice to respondent. 3. Interim maintenance hearing. 4. Final maintenance order.',
     'https://www.indiacode.nic.in/handle/123456789/16225', 'Ministry of Law and Justice, Govt. of India', 11),

    (602, 6, 'Child Custody Governed by Best Interest of the Child Doctrine', 'child-custody-best-interest-doctrine',
     'Custody and guardianship disputes are decided strictly based on the welfare and best interest of the child, not parental rights.',
     'In parental separation, courts do not treat children as property. The parent who can provide better emotional, moral, and educational care gets primary custody, with visitation for the other.',
     'Under Guardians and Wards Act 1890 Sec 17 and Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act 1956 Sec 13, the welfare of the minor child is the paramount consideration.',
     'Parents and legal guardians involved in separation or custody litigation.',
     'Protects child psychological well-being and stability.',
     'Father claimed sole custody citing higher income. The court awarded primary custody to the mother based on the 4-year-old emotional bond and granted the father weekend visitation rights.',
     '1. File petition for custody/guardianship under Guardians and Wards Act.\n2. Emphasize child schooling, emotional stability, and family environment.\n3. Request interim visitation rights to maintain parental bonding.\n4. Court may interview child in chamber before deciding.',
     'Child birth certificate, School records and report cards, Medical history records, Financial capability proofs.',
     'Family Court / Principal Senior Civil Judge', 'eCourts Family Court Wing',
     '1. Custody petition filed. 2. Mediation referral. 3. Interim visitation arrangement. 4. Final custody and parenting plan order.',
     'https://www.indiacode.nic.in/handle/123456789/2288', 'Ministry of Law and Justice, Govt. of India', 19),

    (603, 6, 'Right to Mutual Consent Divorce Without Protracted Delay', 'mutual-consent-divorce-speedy-cooling-waiver',
     'Couples mutually agreeing to separate can obtain divorce within 6 months, and the 6-month cooling period can be waived.',
     'If both husband and wife mutually agree that the marriage cannot work, they can file a joint petition for divorce and settle custody and alimony amicably.',
     'Under Section 13B of Hindu Marriage Act 1955 and Section 28 of Special Marriage Act 1954, mutual consent divorce requires 1-year separation. Supreme Court in Amardeep Singh v. Harveen Kaur held the 6-month cooling period can be waived.',
     'Legally married couples mutually agreeing to dissolve marriage.',
     'Enables dignified separation without protracted, acrimonious litigation.',
     'Couples living separately for 2 years agreed on alimony and assets. They filed a joint mutual consent petition and obtained cooling period waiver, finalizing divorce in 3 weeks.',
     '1. Prepare comprehensive Memorandum of Settlement detailing alimony and custody.\n2. File Joint Petition for Mutual Consent Divorce (First Motion).\n3. Record statements before the Family Court Judge.\n4. Apply for waiver of 6-month cooling period or move Second Motion after 6 months.',
     'Marriage certificate, Photographs of marriage, Address proofs, Joint settlement agreement, Income affidavits.',
     'Family Court / District Court', 'eCourts Portal (ecourts.gov.in)',
     '1. First Motion filed. 2. Statements recorded. 3. Waiver application (if eligible). 4. Second Motion and final decree of divorce.',
     'https://www.indiacode.nic.in/handle/123456789/1572', 'Ministry of Law and Justice, Govt. of India', 19),

    (604, 6, 'Protection of Matrimonial Residence in Shared Household', 'protection-residence-matrimonial-home',
     'A woman cannot be dispossessed or evicted from her matrimonial shared household by husband or in-laws without due process of law.',
     'Even if the house belongs to your father-in-law or husband, your in-laws cannot throw you out on the street. You have the statutory right to reside in the shared household.',
     'Under Section 17 of PWDVA 2005 and Supreme Court in Satish Chander Ahuja v. Sneha Ahuja (2020), every woman in a domestic relationship has the right to reside in the shared household.',
     'Married women and women in domestic relationships living in matrimonial or shared homes.',
     'Prevents arbitrary marital eviction and destitution.',
     'In-laws locked out daughter-in-law while husband was abroad. Magistrate issued an urgent injunction restraining them from disturbing her peaceful possession.',
     '1. File an urgent application under Section 19 PWDVA before the Magistrate.\n2. Seek protection against eviction and an order for keys/possession.\n3. Request local police protection to prevent forceful lockout.\n4. Magistrate can also direct alternative rented accommodation paid by respondent.',
     'Marriage proof, Domestic Incident Report, Photos showing residence, Police complaint copy (if locked out).',
     'Judicial Magistrate First Class / Family Court', 'District Legal Services Authority (DLSA)',
     '1. Application under Sec 19. 2. Urgent hearing in 48 hours. 3. Protection Officer and Police enforce possession order.',
     'https://www.indiacode.nic.in/handle/123456789/2021', 'Ministry of Women and Child Development, Govt. of India', 14),

    (605, 6, 'Right to Legal Child Adoption Through CARA Framework', 'legal-child-adoption-cara-framework',
     'Prospective parents and children have the legal right to secure, transparent adoption governed by CARA, creating full legal parent-child relationship.',
     'Adopting a child must be done through CARA (Central Adoption Resource Authority). Direct informal adoptions without court/CARA orders are illegal and punishable.',
     'Under Juvenile Justice Act 2015 Sections 56-73, all adoptions in India must comply with CARA regulations. A finalized adoption order makes the adopted child equal in inheritance and legal rights as a biological child.',
     'Prospective adoptive parents (married couples or single individuals) meeting eligibility criteria.',
     'Protects child welfare, prevents child trafficking, and gives undeniable legal identity.',
     'Rohan and Neha registered on CARINGS portal. After home study by Specialized Adoption Agency and District Magistrate approval, they legally welcomed a 2-year-old child with full inheritance rights.',
     '1. Register online on CARINGS portal (cara.wcd.gov.in).\n2. Specialized Adoption Agency (SAA) conducts Home Study Report (HSR).\n3. Child referral and reserve matching based on preferences.\n4. District Magistrate issues final legal adoption order.',
     'Birth certificates, Marriage certificate (if couple), Medical fitness certificates, Income tax returns (3 years), Police verification report.',
     'Central Adoption Resource Authority (CARA) / District Magistrate', 'CARINGS Portal (cara.wcd.gov.in)',
     '1. Online registration. 2. Home study approval. 3. Child matching. 4. Pre-adoption foster care. 5. DM adoption order.',
     'https://cara.wcd.gov.in', 'Ministry of Women and Child Development, Govt. of India', 20),

    # Cat 7: Consumer Rights (id: 7)
    (701, 7, 'Right to Return Defective Goods & Full Refund', 'right-to-return-defective-goods-and-refund',
     'Consumers have statutory rights to replacement, repair, or full refund for defective goods and deficient commercial services.',
     'When you buy any product and it turns out broken or not as promised, the seller/brand is legally bound to replace it or give a full refund.',
     'Section 2(9) and Section 35 of Consumer Protection Act 2019 grant comprehensive rights against unfair trade practices and product defects. No-refund clauses are void.',
     'All citizens purchasing goods or services for personal consideration online or offline.',
     'Protects consumers from commercial exploitation.',
     'Pooja washing machine broke on Day 2. E-commerce seller refused return. NCH complaint (1915) resulted in full brand replacement in 48 hours.',
     '1. Document defect with photos, unboxing video, and tax invoice.\n2. Lodge complaint with seller in writing.\n3. Call 1915 or register on consumerhelpline.gov.in.\n4. File e-Daakhil consumer complaint before District Commission if unresolved.',
     'Tax invoice, Warranty card, Photos/videos of defect, Merchant chat transcripts.',
     'District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (DCDRC)', 'National Consumer Helpline (1915 / consumerhelpline.gov.in)',
     '1. NCH grievance. 2. Brand mediation. 3. If failed, e-Daakhil complaint. 4. Commission orders refund plus compensation.',
     'https://consumerhelpline.gov.in', 'Department of Consumer Affairs, Govt. of India', 2),

    (702, 7, 'Protection Against Dark Patterns & Misleading E-Commerce Ads', 'dark-patterns-and-misleading-advertisements',
     'Consumers are protected from manipulative digital design tricks like drip pricing, forced add-ons, and fake urgency.',
     'Websites cannot trick you into buying subscriptions, add hidden fees at the final payment screen, or use countdown timers to deceive you.',
     'Central Consumer Protection Authority (CCPA) Guidelines 2023 define 13 prohibited dark patterns including False Urgency, Basket Sneaking, Confirm Shaming, and Forced Action as unfair trade practices.',
     'All online shoppers and digital app users in India.',
     'Guarantees fair choice and transparent pricing in digital commerce.',
     'Travel booking app sneaked insurance into cart and charged convenience fees at final step. CCPA complaint compelled fee refund and removal of pre-ticked boxes.',
     '1. Take screenshots of deceptive pricing, hidden add-ons, or cancellation hurdles.\n2. Raise written objection with merchant customer care.\n3. Report dark pattern on National Consumer Helpline portal.\n4. CCPA can impose fines up to Rs. 10 Lakhs on offenders.',
     'Screenshots of checkout steps, Final invoice showing hidden charges, App screen recording.',
     'Central Consumer Protection Authority (CCPA)', 'National Consumer Helpline (consumerhelpline.gov.in)',
     '1. Report dark pattern. 2. CCPA notices to platform. 3. Discontinuation of deceptive design. 4. Restitution to affected consumers.',
     'https://consumeraffairs.nic.in', 'Department of Consumer Affairs, Govt. of India', 2),

    (703, 7, 'Right to Compensation for Deficient Commercial Services', 'compensation-for-deficient-services-cpa',
     'Consumers are entitled to compensation for deficiency in banking, courier, aviation, insurance, and telecommunication services.',
     'If an airline loses your luggage, a courier misplaces important documents, or a bank wrongfully delays transactions, you can claim damages.',
     'Section 2(11) of Consumer Protection Act 2019 defines deficiency as any fault, imperfection, or inadequacy in service quality, entitling consumers to compensation.',
     'All individuals paying consideration for professional, financial, or commercial services.',
     'Enforces service quality and accountability across service sectors.',
     'Courier lost original educational certificates sent for job interview. Consumer Commission awarded Rs. 1.2L compensation for mental agony and loss of opportunity.',
     '1. Preserve consignment note, booking receipt, and service agreement.\n2. Send formal written demand letter to customer care giving 15 days.\n3. Lodge grievance on National Consumer Helpline (1915).\n4. File claim on e-Daakhil before District Consumer Commission.',
     'Booking receipt, Service contract, Proof of damage/loss, Written grievance correspondence.',
     'District Consumer Commission (DCDRC) / Sectoral Ombudsman', 'e-Daakhil Portal (edaakhil.nic.in)',
     '1. Notice to provider. 2. e-Daakhil filing. 3. Commission summons respondent. 4. Award of refund and damages.',
     'https://edaakhil.nic.in', 'National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (NCDRC)', 2),

    (704, 7, 'Strict Product Liability Redressal Against Manufacturers', 'strict-product-liability-cpa-2019',
     'Manufacturers and sellers are strictly liable to compensate consumers for physical harm or property damage caused by dangerous or flawed products.',
     'If an appliance explodes, food product causes poisoning, or phone battery catches fire, the manufacturer must pay for all injuries and property damages.',
     'Chapter VI (Sections 84-87) of Consumer Protection Act 2019 introduced statutory Product Liability. Strict liability applies without needing to prove manufacturer negligence.',
     'Any consumer or bystander injured by defective products.',
     'Ensures industrial safety standards and holds global manufacturers accountable.',
     'A smartphone battery exploded while charging, injuring a student hand. The Consumer Commission awarded Rs. 5.5L product liability compensation against the manufacturer.',
     '1. Preserve defective product, original packaging, and purchase invoice.\n2. Document injuries with hospital records and doctor certificates.\n3. Take photographs of damaged property.\n4. File product liability claim before Consumer Commission.',
     'Purchase invoice, Physical defective item, Medical records and doctor certificates, Photos of damage.',
     'Consumer Commission / Central Consumer Protection Authority', 'e-Daakhil Portal',
     '1. File claim with medical proof. 2. Laboratory testing of product. 3. Strict liability adjudication. 4. Compensation award.',
     'https://consumeraffairs.nic.in', 'Department of Consumer Affairs, Govt. of India', 2),

    (705, 7, 'Right to File Online Consumer Case via e-Daakhil Without Lawyer', 'file-consumer-complaint-edaakhil-without-lawyer',
     'Citizens can file and argue consumer cases online from home using the e-Daakhil portal without needing an advocate.',
     'You do not need to hire a lawyer to fight for your consumer rights. You can draft your own complaint, pay a nominal online court fee, and attend virtual hearings.',
     'Consumer Protection Act 2019 and e-Daakhil system empower consumers to file complaints electronically from anywhere, pay online fees, and represent themselves as party-in-person.',
     'Any aggrieved consumer seeking dispute redressal up to Rs. 50 Lakhs (District Commission).',
     'Democratizes justice and eliminates advocate dependency for small claims.',
     'A citizen denied a Rs. 12,000 hotel refund filed an e-Daakhil petition himself in 20 minutes, won the case in 3 hearings, and recovered the full refund plus Rs. 5,000 costs.',
     '1. Register as Citizen on edaakhil.nic.in using Aadhaar/PAN.\n2. Draft simple facts: what happened, defect/deficiency, relief sought.\n3. Upload invoice and correspondence in PDF.\n4. Pay nominal court fee via Bharatkosh and track hearing dates.',
     'Purchase invoice / Payment proof, Written notice to seller, Written reply (if any), Aadhaar ID copy.',
     'District Consumer Commission', 'e-Daakhil Portal (edaakhil.nic.in)',
     '1. Online registration. 2. Upload petition. 3. Scrutiny in 48 hours. 4. Notice issued. 5. Virtual or physical hearing.',
     'https://edaakhil.nic.in', 'NCDRC, Department of Consumer Affairs', 2),

    # Cat 8: Cybercrime & Digital Rights (id: 8)
    (801, 8, 'Protection Against Online Financial Fraud and 1930 Helpline Interception', 'protection-against-online-financial-fraud',
     'Victims of unauthorized digital transactions are entitled to immediate transaction freezing via 1930 and bank refund under RBI rules.',
     'If someone steals money from your bank account through UPI fraud or phishing, reporting it on Helpline 1930 within the golden hour freezes the money in transit.',
     'RBI Circular on Limiting Customer Liability and IT Act Sec 66D establish zero customer liability if reported within 3 days. I4C platform intercepts fund routing.',
     'All bank account holders, UPI users, and cardholders in India.',
     'Protects citizens against cyber financial loss.',
     'Arjun lost Rs. 75,000 in an electricity bill SMS phishing scam. Calling 1930 in 40 minutes intercepted the fraud account and reversed the entire amount.',
     '1. Call National Cyber Helpline 1930 immediately.\n2. Block card/account via banking app or phone.\n3. Register full incident report on cybercrime.gov.in.\n4. Submit copy of Cyber FIR Acknowledgement to home branch within 3 days.',
     'Bank statement showing debit, Screenshot of fraud SMS/URL, Transaction Reference (UTR/UPI Ref), Cyber Crime Acknowledgement.',
     'Indian Cyber Crime Coordination Centre (I4C) & Bank Ombudsman', 'National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal (cybercrime.gov.in)',
     '1. Dial 1930 to freeze funds. 2. File cybercrime.gov.in report. 3. Branch submission. 4. RBI zero-liability refund.',
     'https://cybercrime.gov.in', 'Ministry of Home Affairs, Govt. of India', 4),

    (802, 8, 'Protection Against Non-Consensual Image Leaks & Cyberstalking', 'cyberstalking-and-nonconsensual-image-protection',
     'Victims of online stalking, morphing, or non-consensual image sharing have the right to mandatory 24-hour takedown and criminal prosecution.',
     'If anyone shares private photos, morphs pictures, or stalks you online, police must register an FIR and social media platforms must remove the content within 24 hours.',
     'Section 66E/67A of IT Act 2000 and IT Rules 2021 mandate social media intermediaries to take down non-consensual intimate imagery within 24 hours of notification.',
     'All individuals facing online harassment, morphing, blackmails, or digital stalking.',
     'Protects bodily privacy and personal dignity under Article 21.',
     'A woman was blackmailed by an ex-partner threatening to post private photos. Cyber police registered an FIR and issued an emergency takedown notice, purging all links within 12 hours.',
     '1. Do not delete chats, usernames, profile URLs, or timestamps.\n2. Report immediately under "Report Cybercrime Against Women/Children" on cybercrime.gov.in.\n3. Submit report to social media platform Grievance Officer.\n4. Call 1930 or visit local Cyber Police Station for FIR.',
     'Screenshots of harassing messages, Account handles/URLs, Message headers, Incident chronology.',
     'Cyber Crime Police Station / Platform Grievance Officer', 'National Cybercrime Portal / StopNCII.org',
     '1. Emergency portal report. 2. Intermediary 24-hr takedown. 3. Cyber FIR registration. 4. Accused arrest and digital forensics.',
     'https://cybercrime.gov.in', 'Ministry of Home Affairs / MeitY', 4),

    (803, 8, 'Right to Erasure, Correction & Grievance Redressal Under DPDP Act', 'dpdp-act-data-erasure-and-correction',
     'Citizens have statutory rights to view what personal data apps hold, correct mistakes, and withdraw consent demanding data erasure.',
     'You have the legal right to ask companies what data they have about you, correct wrong details, and delete your account and personal history permanently.',
     'Sections 12 and 13 of the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 grant Data Principals enforceable rights to access, correction, erasure, and grievance redressal against Data Fiduciaries.',
     'All digital citizens whose personal data is processed by apps, websites, and companies.',
     'Restores personal data ownership and privacy autonomy.',
     'An e-commerce platform refused to delete credit card information and phone records after account closure. Notice citing DPDP Act compelled complete data deletion within 7 days.',
     '1. Review app privacy settings and consent dashboards.\n2. Submit formal Data Correction or Erasure request to Data Protection Officer (DPO).\n3. Company must resolve request within prescribed statutory period.\n4. Escalate to Data Protection Board of India if company refuses.',
     'Copy of written erasure request to DPO, App account details, Company response/rejection.',
     'Data Protection Board of India / Company DPO', 'Data Protection Board Online Portal',
     '1. Request to company DPO. 2. Company response within 30 days. 3. If dissatisfied, appeal to Data Protection Board. 4. Penalties on company.',
     'https://www.meity.gov.in', 'Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology, Govt. of India', 12),

    (804, 8, 'Protection Against SIM Swapping and Unauthorized Mobile Connections', 'sim-swapping-and-sanchar-saathi-tafcop',
     'Citizens have the right to check all SIM cards issued in their name and block unauthorized numbers or stolen mobile handsets.',
     'You can check how many mobile numbers are active in your name using the government Sanchar Saathi portal, and block unknown SIM cards immediately.',
     'Department of Telecommunications (DoT) Sanchar Saathi initiative (TAFCOP and CEIR modules) provides real-time tracking of mobile numbers registered against a citizen Aadhaar and blocks stolen devices.',
     'All mobile subscribers and telecom consumers in India.',
     'Prevents identity theft, fraud calls, and SIM-swap banking attacks.',
     'Suresh checked TAFCOP and found two unknown active SIM cards registered under his Aadhaar in another state. He reported them online, leading to immediate deactivation by telecom operators.',
     '1. Visit Sanchar Saathi portal (sancharsaathi.gov.in).\n2. Use TAFCOP module to view all mobile numbers registered with your ID.\n3. Flag numbers you do not recognize as "Not My Number".\n4. Use CEIR module to block IMEI of lost or stolen handsets.',
     'Aadhaar number, Active registered mobile number for OTP verification, Lost phone IMEI number (if stolen).',
     'Department of Telecommunications (DoT) / Telecom Service Providers', 'Sanchar Saathi Portal (sancharsaathi.gov.in)',
     '1. Check numbers on TAFCOP. 2. Flag unauthorized SIMs. 3. Telecom operator verification. 4. Permanent disconnection.',
     'https://sancharsaathi.gov.in', 'Department of Telecommunications, Govt. of India', 4),

    (805, 8, 'Protection Against Harassment by Illegal Digital Lending Apps', 'illegal-loan-app-harassment-protection',
     'Borrowers are legally protected from aggressive recovery tactics, contact list hacking, and morphed photo extortion by digital loan apps.',
     'Online loan apps cannot access your phone gallery or contacts, cannot call your relatives, and cannot harass you with abusive messages.',
     'RBI Digital Lending Guidelines 2022 prohibit lending apps from accessing mobile storage, contact lists, or media. Loan recovery must follow Fair Practices Code; harassment is a punishable crime.',
     'All borrowers and smartphone users targeted by unregulated instant loan apps.',
     'Protects privacy, reputation, and mental health against predatory digital extortion.',
     'Ramesh was harassed by an unregistered loan app calling friends from his contact list. Lodging a complaint on cybercrime.gov.in and RBI Sachet portal resulted in the app developer being booked and calls ceasing.',
     '1. Do not pay extortion demands under duress.\n2. File a complaint on cybercrime.gov.in under financial fraud and extortion.\n3. Report the app on RBI Sachet portal (sachet.rbi.org.in).\n4. Lodge police complaint for extortion under Section 384 IPC / BNS.',
     'Screenshots of abusive chats and call recordings, App transaction details, Bank account statements.',
     'Cyber Police / Reserve Bank of India (RBI)', 'RBI Sachet Portal (sachet.rbi.org.in)',
     '1. File cyber police report. 2. Report on Sachet. 3. Bank accounts of fraudulent lending syndicates frozen. 4. App taken down from store.',
     'https://sachet.rbi.org.in', 'Reserve Bank of India', 4),

    # Cat 9: Government Services & Citizen Rights (id: 9)
    (901, 9, 'Right to Information from Public Authorities Within 30 Days (RTI)', 'right-to-information-rti-30-days',
     'Citizens have the statutory right to request and inspect government files, tender records, and scheme expenditures within 30 days.',
     'Under the RTI Act, any citizen can ask government departments questions about delayed projects, road repairs, or fund utilization. The department must answer in 30 days (48 hours for life/liberty).',
     'Sections 6 and 7 of the Right to Information Act, 2005 mandate timely disposal of citizen queries. Failure attracts penalty of Rs. 250/day up to Rs. 25,000 on the Public Information Officer.',
     'All Indian citizens seeking public accountability and government transparency.',
     'Enforces democratic transparency and participatory governance.',
     'Residents filed an RTI asking why a sanctioned 2-km link road was unpaved for 18 months. The municipality responded with tender timelines and initiated road paving within 3 weeks.',
     '1. Draft specific, clear questions on facts/records (not opinions or advice).\n2. File online via rtionline.gov.in or submit physical application to Central/State PIO.\n3. Pay statutory fee of Rs. 10 (free for BPL citizens).\n4. If no reply in 30 days, file First Appeal within 30 days.',
     'RTI application form, Identity proof (BPL card if fee exempt), Rs. 10 fee receipt / IPO.',
     'Public Information Officer (PIO) / Central Information Commission (CIC)', 'RTI Online Portal (rtionline.gov.in)',
     '1. Submit RTI. 2. PIO response in 30 days. 3. If dissatisfied, First Appeal to FAA. 4. Second Appeal to Information Commission.',
     'https://rtionline.gov.in', 'Department of Personnel and Training (DoPT), Govt. of India', 9),

    (902, 9, 'Right to Guaranteed Time-Bound Delivery of Public Services', 'guaranteed-time-bound-public-services',
     'Citizens are entitled to guaranteed timelines for caste, income, domicile certificates, electricity meters, and building approvals.',
     'State Public Service Guarantee Acts mandate that local officials must issue certificates or licenses within set days. If delayed, you can claim compensation from the defaulting official.',
     'State Right to Public Services Legislation (e.g. Sakala in Karnataka, RTS in Maharashtra, e-District) prescribes maximum statutory delivery times and automatic penalties for delinquent officers.',
     'All citizens applying for state certificates, revenue extracts, utility connections, and civic licenses.',
     'Eliminates bureaucratic delays and reduces petty bribery.',
     'Sanjay applied for a caste certificate with a 15-day statutory SLA. When delayed on Day 18, an appeal under the RTS Act compelled issuance in 24 hours and a notice to the tehsildar.',
     '1. Apply through state online service portal (e-District / MeeSeva / Seva Sindhu).\n2. Note down the unique Application Acknowledgment Number and SLA due date.\n3. If SLA breached, file First Appeal before the designated Appellate Authority.\n4. Second Appeal can award compensation up to Rs. 5,000 deducted from officer salary.',
     'Application acknowledgment receipt, Identity proof, Address proof, Required domain-specific supporting documents.',
     'Designated Public Service Officer / District Collector', 'State Citizen Service Portal (e-District / ServicePlus)',
     '1. Submit application. 2. SLA countdown. 3. If breached, auto-escalation or First Appeal. 4. Guaranteed delivery with compensation.',
     'https://serviceonline.gov.in', 'National Informatics Centre / Ministry of Electronics & IT', 9),

    (903, 9, 'Right to Grievance Redressal via CPGRAMS Portal', 'cpgrams-central-public-grievance-redressal',
     'Citizens have the right to lodge and track grievances against central and state ministries on CPGRAMS with mandatory 30-day resolution.',
     'If a bank, railway, post office, or central ministry ignores your problem, you can lodge a grievance directly on CPGRAMS. It is tracked by top authorities with a 30-day resolution mandate.',
     'Centralized Public Grievance Redress and Monitoring System (CPGRAMS) operated by DARPG provides single-window redressal across 80+ central ministries with appeal facility to an independent Nodal Officer.',
     'All citizens facing service deficiency from central and participating state government departments.',
     'Provides direct executive oversight on citizen complaints.',
     'A pensioner pension was blocked due to an inter-bank clerical mismatch for 5 months. Within 12 days of filing a CPGRAMS grievance, the Pension Department credited all arrears.',
     '1. Register on CPGRAMS portal (pgportal.gov.in) using mobile number.\n2. Select relevant Ministry, Department, and sub-category.\n3. Provide concise summary of grievance and upload previous correspondence.\n4. If unsatisfied with resolution, file an Appeal within 30 days.',
     'Previous complaint reference numbers, Government department communication, Identity proof, Supporting evidence.',
     'Department of Administrative Reforms and Public Grievances (DARPG)', 'CPGRAMS Portal (pgportal.gov.in)',
     '1. File grievance. 2. Assigned to ministry nodal officer. 3. Action taken report in 30 days. 4. Feedback rating and appeal option.',
     'https://pgportal.gov.in', 'DARPG, Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances & Pensions', 9),

    (904, 9, 'Right to Biometric Lock & Demographic Correction in Aadhaar', 'aadhaar-biometric-lock-and-demographic-correction',
     'Aadhaar holders have the statutory right to lock biometrics to prevent cloning and correct demographic errors online.',
     'You can lock your fingerprint and iris authentication on the mAadhaar app so no one can misuse your Aadhaar for bank debits or SIM issuance. You can unlock it instantly when needed.',
     'Under the Aadhaar (Targeted Delivery of Financial and Other Subsidies) Act 2016 and UIDAI regulations, citizens have full control to lock/unlock biometrics and update demographic data with documentary proof.',
     'All 1.4+ billion Indian residents holding a 12-digit Aadhaar number.',
     'Protects identity security and biometric integrity against AePS fraud.',
     'Anita locked her biometrics on mAadhaar after hearing of AePS fraud in her town. When an attempted unauthorized biometric withdrawal was made at a CSP, it was automatically declined.',
     '1. Download official mAadhaar app or visit myaadhaar.uidai.gov.in.\n2. Login with Aadhaar and OTP.\n3. Select "Lock/Unlock Biometrics" and enable lock.\n4. Temporarily unlock for 10 minutes when giving biometrics for exams or bank visits.',
     'Aadhaar number, Registered mobile number active for OTP verification.',
     'Unique Identification Authority of India (UIDAI)', 'myAadhaar Portal (myaadhaar.uidai.gov.in) / Helpline 1947',
     '1. Login to myAadhaar. 2. Enable biometric lock. 3. Real-time protection. 4. File UIDAI grievance via 1947 if needed.',
     'https://uidai.gov.in', 'UIDAI, Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology', 9),

    (905, 9, 'Right to Timely Passport Issuance and Grievance Redressal', 'passport-issuance-and-grievance-redressal',
     'Citizens have the statutory right to travel abroad and receive passport services in transparent, time-bound manner under the Passports Act.',
     'Every Indian citizen has the fundamental right to hold an Indian passport. The government cannot refuse or delay your passport without specific statutory grounds in writing.',
     'Under the Passports Act, 1967 and Supreme Court in Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India, right to travel abroad is part of Article 21 personal liberty. Passports can only be refused for grounds enumerated in Section 6.',
     'All eligible Indian citizens applying for fresh passports, renewals, or Tatkaal services.',
     'Protects international mobility and personal liberty under Article 21.',
     'A student passport was stalled due to an erroneous adverse police report over a closed non-cognizable neighborhood dispute. Representation to the Regional Passport Officer (RPO) resulted in clearance.',
     '1. Submit application on passportindia.gov.in.\n2. Attend biometric appointment at Passport Seva Kendra (PSK).\n3. Track police verification report online.\n4. If stalled, book Passport Adalat appointment or appeal to Regional Passport Officer under Sec 11.',
     'Proof of date of birth (Birth Certificate / 10th marksheet), Proof of address (Aadhaar / Voter ID), Photo ID, Police verification clearance.',
     'Regional Passport Office (RPO) / Ministry of External Affairs', 'Passport Seva Portal (passportindia.gov.in)',
     '1. Online PSK appointment. 2. Police verification within 21 days. 3. Dispatch via Speed Post. 4. Appeal to Chief Passport Officer if refused.',
     'https://www.passportindia.gov.in', 'Ministry of External Affairs, Govt. of India', 9),

    # Cat 10: Legal Aid & Access to Justice (id: 10)
    (1001, 10, 'Right to Free Legal Representation for Weaker Sections', 'right-to-free-legal-aid',
     'Eligible citizens are entitled to free legal counsel, court fee waivers, and representation in all judicial proceedings.',
     'If you cannot afford a private lawyer or belong to eligible categories like women, children, SC/ST, or low-income groups, government provides a qualified lawyer free of charge.',
     'Under Article 39A of the Constitution and Section 12 of Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987, NALSA and DLSAs provide end-to-end legal representation and court fee coverage.',
     'Women, children, SC/ST members, disabled persons, industrial workmen, and citizens with annual income below prescribed thresholds (typically Rs. 3 Lakhs).',
     'Guarantees equal access to justice regardless of economic hardship.',
     'Sunita, facing illegal eviction by landlord, approached DLSA. DLSA assigned a panel advocate free of cost who obtained a stay order protecting her occupancy.',
     '1. Visit the nearest District Legal Services Authority (DLSA) in District Court.\n2. Or apply online via nalsa.gov.in.\n3. Submit income declaration and identity proof.\n4. Meet assigned panel advocate and hand over dispute documents.',
     'Identity proof (Aadhaar / Voter ID), Income certificate or self-declaration, Caste/category certificate (if applicable), Case notices.',
     'National Legal Services Authority (NALSA) / DLSA', 'NALSA Free Legal Aid Portal (nalsa.gov.in)',
     '1. Application submission. 2. Front office scrutiny in 48 hours. 3. Advocate assigned from panel. 4. Representation in court.',
     'https://nalsa.gov.in', 'Supreme Court of India / NALSA', 3),

    (1002, 10, 'Settlement of Disputes Through National Lok Adalat', 'dispute-settlement-national-lok-adalat',
     'Citizens can resolve pending civil, matrimonial, bank loan, and motor accident disputes amicably in Lok Adalat with full refund of court fees.',
     'Lok Adalats offer fast, compromise-based settlements with zero court fees. If you settle a pending court case in Lok Adalat, you get 100% of your paid court fees refunded, and no appeal lies against the award.',
     'Sections 19, 20, and 21 of the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987 give Lok Adalat awards the status of a final civil court decree. Court fees paid in the court are refunded under Section 16 of the Court Fees Act.',
     'All parties involved in compoundable criminal, civil, motor accident, matrimonial, or financial recovery disputes.',
     'Delivers finality without appeals, saves decades of litigation costs.',
     'A 4-year-old motor accident claim was referred to National Lok Adalat. The insurance company and victim agreed on Rs. 8.5L compensation in a single day, and full court fees were refunded.',
     '1. Apply to the presiding judge of your court to refer case to upcoming Lok Adalat.\n2. Or approach DLSA for Pre-Litigation Lok Adalat reference before filing formal suit.\n3. Attend conciliation sitting with panel members (retired judge + advocate).\n4. Sign joint compromise settlement; award is final and non-appealable.',
     'Case number and petition copy, Compromise terms draft, ID proofs, Court fee deposit receipt.',
     'National Legal Services Authority (NALSA) / State Legal Services Authority (SLSA)', 'NALSA National Lok Adalat Schedule',
     '1. Referral to Lok Adalat. 2. Pre-counseling sitting. 3. Joint settlement signed. 4. Award passed and court fee refund certificate issued.',
     'https://nalsa.gov.in', 'Supreme Court of India / NALSA', 3),

    (1003, 10, 'Pre-Litigation Mediation Under the Mediation Act, 2023', 'pre-litigation-mediation-act-2023',
     'Parties in civil and commercial disputes have the right to resolve conflicts confidentially through accredited mediators before going to court.',
     'Instead of fighting in court for years, you can sit with a trained neutral mediator to work out a mutually acceptable settlement. The signed mediated agreement has the same legal value as a court judgment.',
     'The Mediation Act, 2023 formalizes pre-litigation mediation. A Mediated Settlement Agreement is enforceable in the same manner as a judgment or decree passed by a court under CPC.',
     'Parties to commercial, property, family, partnership, and civil disputes.',
     'Preserves relationships, ensures confidentiality, and dramatically lowers dispute costs.',
     'Two business partners disputing asset division entered pre-litigation mediation. Over 4 sessions, the mediator helped them structure an asset buyout, registered as an enforceable decree in 20 days.',
     '1. Approach a court-annexed Mediation Centre or recognized Mediation Service Provider.\n2. Serve mediation notice to the opposite party.\n3. Attend confidential joint and separate caucus sessions with the mediator.\n4. Execute Mediated Settlement Agreement and register it with the Authority.',
     'Dispute summary statement, Relevant contracts/agreements, Identity proofs, Draft terms of settlement.',
     'Mediation and Conciliation Project Committee (Supreme Court) / District Court Mediation Centre', 'Mediation Council of India Portal',
     '1. Mediation notice. 2. Mediator appointment. 3. Maximum 120-day mediation process. 4. Enforceable settlement agreement executed.',
     'https://www.indiacode.nic.in/handle/123456789/19324', 'Ministry of Law and Justice, Govt. of India', 3),

    (1004, 10, 'Right to Free Certified Copies of Court Orders for Indigent Litigants', 'free-certified-copies-court-orders',
     'Litigants belonging to indigent categories or represented by legal aid have the right to free certified copies of judgments and trial orders.',
     'If you are represented by legal aid or in custody, the court must give you certified copies of bail orders, chargesheets, and judgments free of charge.',
     'Under High Court Rules and Section 363 CrPC (Sec 404 BNSS), an accused person sentenced to imprisonment or a legal-aid litigant is entitled to a free certified copy of judgment immediately upon pronouncement.',
     'All indigent litigants, prisoners, undertrials, and parties represented by DLSA/HCLSC.',
     'Guarantees fair appeal opportunities and prevents incarceration without judicial record.',
     'An undertrial in judicial custody was granted bail but could not afford certified copy copying fees. Legal aid counsel applied under Section 363 CrPC and obtained the certified order free, releasing him.',
     '1. File an urgent application for certified copy marked "Through Legal Aid / In Custody".\n2. Cite Section 363 CrPC / Sec 404 BNSS and DLSA representation.\n3. Copying agency must deliver certified copy on prioritized basis without charging court fee stamps.\n4. Furnish copy to Jail Superintendent for bail release.',
     'Case number / Court room details, DLSA allotment letter, Undertrial custody certificate.',
     'Court Copying Agency / District Legal Services Authority', 'eCourts Portal (ecourts.gov.in)',
     '1. File free copy application. 2. Judicial officer endorsement. 3. Prioritized copying. 4. Certified delivery to litigant/jail.',
     'https://ecourts.gov.in', 'e-Committee, Supreme Court of India', 3),

    (1005, 10, 'Right to State-Appointed Defense Counsel in Criminal Trials', 'state-appointed-defense-counsel-criminal-trial',
     'Every accused person unable to afford an advocate is entitled to a qualified defense counsel appointed and paid by the state.',
     'If you are arrested and cannot pay for a lawyer, the Magistrate or Sessions Court must assign a qualified advocate to defend you at state expense.',
     'Article 22(1) and Article 39A of Constitution, read with Section 304 CrPC (Sec 340 BNSS) and landmark Supreme Court ruling in Khatri v. State of Bihar, make legal representation an inalienable right from the moment of arrest.',
     'All accused persons facing criminal prosecution who lack financial means to engage private counsel.',
     'Prevents unfair convictions and protects liberty against arbitrary state power.',
     'A daily wage worker was falsely implicated in a theft case and produced before the Magistrate. The Magistrate noted he had no counsel and immediately assigned a DLSA panel advocate, who secured bail in the first remand hearing.',
     '1. Inform the Remand Magistrate immediately that you cannot afford a private lawyer.\n2. Magistrate has a constitutional duty to assign a DLSA Remand Advocate.\n3. Request interaction with legal-aid counsel before remand order is signed.\n4. Counsel files bail application and defends trial free of cost.',
     'Identity proof (if available), Remand paper copy, DLSA appointment form.',
     'Judicial Magistrate / Sessions Court / DLSA Legal Aid Defense Counsel System (LADCS)', 'NALSA LADCS Wing',
     '1. Production before Magistrate. 2. Legal aid inquiry by Bench. 3. LADCS advocate assigned on spot. 4. Defense representation throughout trial.',
     'https://nalsa.gov.in', 'Supreme Court of India / NALSA', 3)
]
